ility / Selection Criteria:**\nCCD is particularly useful when:\n- The researcher seeks to optimize a response variable influenced by multiple factors.\n- The number of factors is moderate (typically 3 to 5).\n- A preliminary understanding of the factor effects is available, allowing for the selection of appropriate levels.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of a CCD typically involves:\n1. Fitting a second-order polynomial model to the data using regression analysis.\n2. Conducting ANOVA to assess the significance of the model and individual factors.\n3. Performing diagnostic checks on residuals to validate model assumptions.\n4. Using response surface plots to visualize the effects and identify optimal conditions.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- CCD allows for efficient exploration of the response surface with fewer runs than a full factorial design.\n- It provides a robust framework for estimating curvature in the response surface.\n- The design is flexible and can be adapted to various experimental conditions.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- CCD may not be suitable for highly complex systems with many interactions or non-linearities beyond quadratic.\n- The design requires careful selection of the axial distance \\( \\alpha \\) to ensure adequate exploration of the response surface.\n- It may require a larger number of experimental runs compared to simpler designs if the number of factors increases.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nAlternatives to CCD include Box-Behnken designs and full factorial designs. Each has its own strengths and weaknesses depending on the specific experimental context and objectives.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing a CCD, researchers should consider:\n- The selection of factor levels and the range of each factor.\n- The need for randomization to mitigate bias.\n- The replication of center points to assess experimental error.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nStatisti